  "slug": "disease-protein-expands-80c4",
  "summary": "Recent preprints quantify how training data, tissue sampling and graph normalisation alter genetic and transcriptomic inference results.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that computational target inference is sensitive to how biological context and network structure are encoded. BRIDGE propagates genetic evidence through protein interaction and disease-similarity networks, expanding genetic support for target-indication pairs. DEFA shows that GWAS effector gene prioritisation performance and drug-target recovery depend on the domain of the training data, while SigMatch shows that sample tissue strongly shapes transcriptomic disease relationships. Inverse Node Frequency reduces hub bias in knowledge graph metapath features, improving target-adverse event prediction. What is new here is the explicit measurement of how context and normalisation change results. SNOWS (glioma stratification, C-index 0.691 to 0.712 in an independent cohort) and the 3D facial benchmark address different clinical questions and are included for completeness rather than as part of this thread.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
